content"><div class=3D"no-overflow"><h2>Overview</h2>
<p>The commentaries and study tasks in Module 2 focused on character and ch=
aracterization. This module covers setting and the interaction between sett=
ing and character. Once again, you=E2=80=99ll have opportunities to test yo=
ur understanding of these new concepts through some study questions.</p>
<p>The Module 1 and Module 2 writing tasks and their readings from your wri=
ting handbook have prepared you for your first formal essay assignment. As =
further preparation for your essay, this module contains a final reading an=
d exercise on writing well-developed paragraphs.</p>
<h3>Readings</h3>
<div class=3D"noteInsetbox">
<h5 class=3D"noteTitle">Note</h5>
<p>All the short story readings are from <em>The Norton Anthology of Short =
Fiction</em>, edited by R. V. Cassill and Richard Bausch. All the readings =
on writing are from <em>The Bare Essentials.</em></p>
</div>
<h4>Short Stories</h4>
<p>Margaret Atwood: =E2=80=9CDeath by Landscape=E2=80=9D</p>
<p>Doris Lessing: =E2=80=9CTo Room Nineteen=E2=80=9D</p>
<h4>Writing</h4>
<p>Chapter 27: "Paragraphs"</p>
<h3>Outcomes</h3>
<p>The goals listed in the Course Guide apply to Module 3. After you comple=
te your work for Module 3, you should also be able to do the following:</p>
<div class=3D"listBlock">
<ul>
<li class=3D"listItemNormalBullets">
<p>Identify the key components of setting=E2=80=94physical location, social=
 or cultural circumstances (morals, customs), and time period=E2=80=94as el=
ements of fiction.</p>
</li>
<li class=3D"listItemNormalBullets">
<p>Identify some of the key ways in which character and setting interact in=
 fiction.</p>
</li>
<li class=3D"listItemNormalBullets">
<p>Write paragraphs that contain details and examples (either paraphrase or=
 direct quotation) to support the argument in your critical essays.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</div></div></div></div>
